          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What TSH range does guidance describe as the treatment threshold in subclinical hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "Guidance agrees on one point. Treatment is broadly accepted when thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) stays persistently above about **10 mIU/L** (Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, \"Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change\").\n\nBetween the upper limit of the normal range and 10 mIU/L, authorities disagree:\n- Some guidance suggests a trial of treatment if symptoms, positive thyroid antibodies, or cardiovascular risk are present.\n- Other guidance suggests monitoring alone for most people. It cites trials in older adults that found no symptom benefit from treatment.\n- Some bodies also argue the normal range should be higher for older adults, since a mildly raised value may be normal at that age.\n\nPregnancy is handled separately, with trimester-specific ranges and a lower threshold for treatment. The retrieved passages do not give those pregnancy numbers, so I cannot quote them.\n\nOn evidence strength, the retrieved material does not grade how strong the trial evidence is, and it does not give a full list of trials. The only trial finding it mentions is the older-adult result showing no symptom benefit. For a clearer picture, check the original guideline documents directly.\n\nIf you are wondering about your own TSH result, a clinician who knows your history is the right person to interpret it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
